<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line ltx_leqno">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">MSAO: Adaptive Modality Sparsity-Aware Offloading with Edge-Cloud Collaboration for Efficient Multimodal LLM Inference</h1>
<span class="ltx_pubnotes ltx_pubnotes_meta"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_doi"><span class="ltx_note_name">DOI: </span><a href="https://doi.org/XXXXXXX.XXXXXXX">XXXXXXX.XXXXXXX</a></span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_conference"><span class="ltx_note_name">Conference: </span>Make sure to enter the correct
conference title from your rights confirmation email; June 03–05,
2026; Woodstock, NY</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_isbn"><span class="ltx_note_name">ISBN: </span>978-1-4503-XXXX-X/2026/06</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_ccs"><span class="ltx_note_name">CCS: </span>Information systems</span><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_ccs"><span class="ltx_note_name">CCS: </span>Computer systems organization Data flow architectures</span></span></span>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zheming Yang<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>,
Qi Guo<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">1,2</sup>,
Jun Wan<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>,
Jiarui Ruan<sup id="id4" class="ltx_sup">4</sup>,
Yunqing Hu<sup id="id5" class="ltx_sup">1,2</sup>,
Chang Zhao<sup id="id6" class="ltx_sup">1,2</sup>, and
Xiangyang Li<sup id="id7" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><sup id="id8" class="ltx_sup">1</sup><span id="id9" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">Institute of Computing Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences</span><sup id="id10" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>, <span id="id11" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">University of Chinese Academy of Sciences</span><sup id="id12" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>, <span id="id13" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">University of Science and Technology of China</span><sup id="id14" class="ltx_sup">4</sup>, <span id="id15" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_institution">University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign</span><span id="id16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;"> , <span id="id16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_affiliation_country">China</span></span>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) enable powerful cross-modal reasoning capabilities but impose substantial computational and latency burdens, posing critical challenges for deployment on resource-constrained edge devices. In this paper, we propose MSAO, an adaptive modality sparsity-aware offloading framework with edge-cloud collaboration for efficient MLLM Inference. First, a lightweight heterogeneous modality-aware via fine-grained sparsity module performs spatial-temporal-modal joint analysis to compute the Modality Activation Sparsity (MAS) metric, which quantifies the necessity of each modality with minimal computational overhead. Second, an adaptive speculative edge-cloud collaborative offloading mechanism dynamically schedules workloads between edge and cloud based on the derived MAS scores and real-time system states, leveraging confidence-guided speculative execution to hide communication latency. Extensive experiments on VQAv2 and MMBench benchmarks demonstrate that MSAO achieves a 30% reduction in end-to-end latency and 30%–65% decrease in resource overhead, while delivering a throughput improvement of 1.5<math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> to 2.3<math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> compared to traditional approaches, all without compromising competitive accuracy.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Multimodal LLM, edge-cloud collaboration,
adaptive offloading, inference optimization
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have recently demonstrated remarkable capabilities in integrating visual, auditory, and textual information, enabling unified reasoning across heterogeneous data sources <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ MSAO: Adaptive Modality Sparsity-Aware Offloading with Edge-Cloud Collaboration for Efficient Multimodal LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. These models are increasingly deployed in latency-sensitive applications ranging from intelligent assistants and autonomous systems to real-time perception pipelines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koh et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. However, their impressive performance comes at substantial computational and memory costs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, as the joint processing of multiple modalities, each with distinct spatial, temporal, and semantic characteristics, exponentially increases the inference burden. Cloud-based solutions suffer from excessive transmission latency and bandwidth contention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. Conversely, edge-only approaches offer lower response time and better data locality but are fundamentally constrained by limited computing capacity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, leading to degraded performance on complex multimodal tasks that require deep cross-modal reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome these limitations, recent research has explored edge–cloud collaborative inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, where lightweight preprocessing is performed on edge devices while computation-intensive reasoning is offloaded to the cloud. Such hybrid solutions balance efficiency and accuracy by distributing workloads across heterogeneous resources <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>. However, existing collaborative frameworks typically adopt uniform offloading policies that treat all modalities equally, overlooking the intrinsic heterogeneity across different input types <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In practice, visual inputs (images and videos) often contain substantial spatial and temporal redundancy, such as irrelevant backgrounds or static scenes, that can be aggressively compressed without compromising task performance<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In contrast, textual and audio inputs may carry semantically dense information that requires high-fidelity processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Miao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Moreover, different modalities exhibit varying sensitivity to inference latency and bandwidth constraints. Ignoring these differences leads to suboptimal workload allocation, inefficient resource utilization, and inconsistent user experience <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. First, uniform offloading policies may waste bandwidth by transmitting redundant visual information that could have been processed or filtered on the edge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hsia et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Second, they may unnecessarily burden the edge device with computationally intensive modalities that would be better handled by the cloud, causing increased latency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we present MSAO, an adaptive modality sparsity-aware offloading framework with edge-cloud collaboration for efficient MLLM Inference. It introduces fine-grained modality sparsity analysis into the offloading decision process, enabling adaptive workload scheduling that balances inference quality, latency, and resource consumption. The main contributions are as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop a lightweight heterogeneous modality-aware via fine-grained sparsity module that performs spatial-temporal-modal joint analysis to compute the Modality Activation Sparsity (MAS) metric. This module quantifies the necessity of each modality with minimal computational overhead, enabling efficient identification of redundant information across image, video, and text inputs on resource-constrained edge devices.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose an adaptive speculative edge-cloud collaborative offloading mechanism that dynamically schedules workloads between edge and cloud based on the derived MAS scores and real-time system states. By leveraging confidence-guided speculative execution, this mechanism effectively hides communication latency behind computation, achieving near-optimal overlap between edge draft generation and cloud verification.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The experimental results demonstrate that MSAO achieves a 30% reduction in end-to-end latency and a 30%–65% decrease in resource overhead, while delivering a throughput improvement of 1.5<math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> to 2.3<math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> compared to traditional approaches, all without compromising competitive accuracy.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02945v1/figure1.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/225;" width="476" height="225" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>The overview of MLLM inference. Heterogeneous inputs (image, video, audio, text) are encoded separately, aligned into a unified token space, and processed by the LLM backbone to generate responses. </figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Some research has focused on optimizing MLLM inference in purely cloud-based or purely edge-based environments. Cloud-centric efforts include adaptive scheduling for edge computing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>, compositional reasoning with structured task relations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and recursive offloading in multi-tier networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Edge-only approaches explore the feasibility of deploying LLMs directly on edge devices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, and heterogeneity-aware distributed inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. To overcome the computational constraints of edge devices, several works have proposed speculative decoding and sparsity-aware acceleration techniques. SLED <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>)</cite> presents a speculative LLM decoding framework for efficient edge serving, enabling faster token generation through draft-then-verify mechanisms. SparCIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> leverages contextual and unstructured bit sparsity to accelerate LLM inference on heterogeneous compute-in-memory accelerators. ExeGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oh et al., 2024</a>)</cite> introduces constraint-aware resource scheduling for LLM inference, optimizing resource allocation under latency and power constraints. Recursive offloading for LLM serving in multi-tier networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> proposes hierarchical offloading strategies that recursively partition inference tasks across network tiers. SpaceServe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> explores spatial multiplexing of complementary encoders and decoders for multimodal LLMs, improving throughput by parallelizing different components of the model. While these approaches have made significant strides, cloud-only solutions suffer from high communication latency, while edge-only methods are constrained by limited model capacity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in edge-cloud collaborative inference have sought to balance the computational demands of MLLMs with the resource constraints of edge devices. Various approaches have been proposed, including semantically enhanced offloading for object detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, scene-aware collaborative frameworks for industrial inspection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian and Yang, 2025</a>)</cite>, and adaptive collaboration for visual detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Beyond industrial applications, researchers have explored cloud-edge architectures for driver assistance systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, collaborative inference for enhanced reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, task-oriented feature compression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>, decoupled video inference offloading <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan and Li, 2026</a>)</cite>, cloud-edge adaptation for mobile networking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, historical interaction reuse for on-device inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and joint offloading with model caching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Despite these advances, existing frameworks predominantly adopt uniform offloading policies that treat all modalities equally, overlooking the intrinsic heterogeneity across different input types. Visual inputs often contain spatial and temporal redundancy that can be aggressively compressed, while textual and audio inputs require high-fidelity processing.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multimodal LLM.</span> MLLMs extend text-only LLMs by integrating heterogeneous modalities such as images, audio, and video into a unified reasoning framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Formally, given an input set of modalities <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{x_{t},x_{v},x_{a}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{x_{t},x_{v},x_{a}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to text, vision, and audio, the model extracts modality-specific features via dedicated encoders <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{m}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{m}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h_{m}=f_{m}(x_{m}),\quad m\in{t,v,a}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{m}=f_{m}(x_{m}),\quad m\in{t,v,a}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">These features are then projected into a shared latent space through a learnable projection function <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="z_{m}=g(h_{m}),\quad m\in{t,v,a}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{m}=g(h_{m}),\quad m\in{t,v,a}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The unified embeddings are subsequently fed into a transformer-based backbone for cross-modal reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ge et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>. Notably, different modalities contribute vastly different numbers of tokens; an image may yield hundreds of visual tokens while a text query contributes only a few, leading to significant heterogeneity in computational cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. This modality-aware heterogeneity motivates adaptive offloading strategies that exploit such variations to improve inference efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Edge-Cloud Architecture.</span> Edge-cloud architectures provide a hierarchical computing paradigm that distributes inference workloads across resource-constrained edge devices and powerful cloud servers, balancing latency, energy efficiency, and computational capacity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Typically, the edge handles lightweight preprocessing and small-scale execution, while the cloud undertakes large-model inference and complex reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tong et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. Based on factors such as modality composition, network bandwidth, and edge resource availability, the system dynamically decides whether to execute locally, offload fully to the cloud, or adopt a hybrid approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. This collaborative design mitigates the limitations of standalone edge inference while reducing the dependency on purely cloud-centric solutions, making it especially suitable for real-time multimodal applications under dynamic conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>The Proposed MSAO Framework</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose MSAO, an adaptive modality sparsity-aware offloading framework designed to enhance the MLLM inference efficiency by edge–cloud collaboration, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4. The Proposed MSAO Framework ‣ MSAO: Adaptive Modality Sparsity-Aware Offloading with Edge-Cloud Collaboration for Efficient Multimodal LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. It primarily consists of two parts: (1) <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Lightweight Heterogeneous Modality-Aware via Fine-Grained Sparsity</span>, and (2) <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Adaptive Speculative Edge-Cloud Collaborative Offloading</span>. The core idea of MSAO is to leverage intrinsic modality sparsity as a guiding signal for adaptive offloading decisions. Unlike traditional approaches that treat all modalities uniformly or rely on heuristic-based compression, MSAO first identifies which parts of the multimodal input are critical for task completion and which are redundant, using a lightweight probing network with minimal computational overhead. Based on this fine-grained sparsity analysis, the framework then employs a confidence-guided speculative execution strategy that adaptively determines whether to execute locally on the edge or offload to the cloud and when to invoke speculative decoding to hide communication latency. By jointly optimizing modality selection, workload partitioning, and execution timing, MSAO achieves a principled tradeoff between inference accuracy, end-to-end latency, and resource consumption.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02945v1/figure2.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/243;" width="476" height="243" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>The overview of the proposed MSAO framework.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Lightweight Heterogeneous Modality-Aware via Fine-Grained Sparsity</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enable efficient edge-cloud collaborative inference for MLLMs, it is essential to first identify the computational redundancy inherent in the input data. Instead of directly processing full multimodal inputs with the heavy MLLM backbone, we propose a lightweight prefix probing network that performs <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fine-grained sparsity modeling</span> across spatial, temporal, and modal dimensions. This module quantifies the necessity of each modality for the given inference task, laying the foundation for subsequent adaptive offloading decisions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1. </span>Spatial Sparsity</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For visual modalities (images and video frames), a significant portion of the input often consists of irrelevant backgrounds that contribute little to the final response. Leveraging the observation that early layers in vision encoders capture spatial structures with minimal computational overhead, we design a lightweight spatial probing head attached to the intermediate feature maps of the vision encoder.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{F}^{(l)}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{F}^{(l)}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times C}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the feature map at layer <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> of the vision encoder. We apply a lightweight convolutional predictor to generate a spatial importance map <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{\text{spatial}}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mtext>spatial</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{\text{spatial}}\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{\text{spatial}}=\sigma\left(\text{Conv}_{1\times 1}\left(\text{AvgPool}(\mathbf{F}^{(l)})\right)\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mtext>spatial</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Conv</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mtext>AvgPool</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐅</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{\text{spatial}}=\sigma\left(\text{Conv}_{1\times 1}\left(\text{AvgPool}(\mathbf{F}^{(l)})\right)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sigmoid activation function. Each entry in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}_{\text{spatial}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐌</mi><mtext>spatial</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}_{\text{spatial}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the importance of the corresponding image patch. We then define the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">spatial sparsity ratio</span> <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\text{spatial}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mtext>spatial</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\text{spatial}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the proportion of patches whose importance falls below a threshold <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\text{spatial}}=\frac{|\{(i,j)\mid\mathbf{M}_{\text{spatial}}^{(i,j)}&lt;\tau_{s}\}|}{H\times W}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mtext>spatial</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐌</mi><mtext>spatial</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\text{spatial}}=\frac{|\{(i,j)\mid\mathbf{M}_{\text{spatial}}^{(i,j)}&lt;\tau_{s}\}|}{H\times W}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Patches with low importance are identified as non-critical backgrounds and can be aggressively pruned or coarsely compressed in downstream processing.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2. </span>Temporal Sparsity</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For video inputs, consecutive frames often exhibit substantial temporal redundancy, especially in static scenes. To capture this, we introduce a temporal probing mechanism that computes the similarity between feature representations of adjacent frames with negligible overhead. Given the feature representations <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math> of frames <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t-1</annotation></semantics></math> extracted from the early layers of the vision encoder, we compute their hash-based similarity using the following locality-sensitive hashing (LSH) approximation:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{sim}_{t}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\mathbb{1}\left[h_{k}(\mathbf{f}_{t})=h_{k}(\mathbf{f}_{t-1})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>sim</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐟</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sim}_{t}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\mathbb{1}\left[h_{k}(\mathbf{f}_{t})=h_{k}(\mathbf{f}_{t-1})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{k}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{k}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th hash function, and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of hash functions. The temporal redundancy score <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for frame <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}=1-\text{sim}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mtext>sim</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}=1-\text{sim}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. A low <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates high redundancy with the previous frame, suggesting that frame <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> can be safely subsampled or represented using only the residual difference.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3. </span>Modal Sparsity</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Different modalities contribute unequally to the final reasoning result depending on the input prompt. For instance, a text-based question about the color of an object may heavily rely on visual information, while a question about audio content may render visual input largely irrelevant. To assess modal importance, we design a lightweight cross-modal probing network that estimates the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">information gain</span> of each modality with respect to the task prompt. Let <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the embedding of the input prompt (e.g., user query), and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the compressed representation of modality <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> extracted from the early layers of the corresponding encoder. We compute the conditional relevance score <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{m}=\text{MLP}\left([\mathbf{p};\mathbf{z}_{m}]\right)\in\mathbb{R}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>MLP</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{m}=\text{MLP}\left([\mathbf{p};\mathbf{z}_{m}]\right)\in\mathbb{R},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\cdot;\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">;</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\cdot;\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes concatenation. The scores are normalized across modalities using a softmax function: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{m}=\frac{\exp(\alpha_{m})}{\sum_{m^{\prime}\in\mathcal{M}}\exp(\alpha_{m^{\prime}})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{m}=\frac{\exp(\alpha_{m})}{\sum_{m^{\prime}\in\mathcal{M}}\exp(\alpha_{m^{\prime}})}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of input modalities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.4. </span>Modal Activation Sparsity (MAS)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the above multidimensional analysis, we propose a unified metric, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Modal Activation Sparsity</span> (MAS), to quantify the overall necessity of each modality in the current inference context. The MAS for modality <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{MAS}_{m}=1-\beta_{m}\cdot\left(1-\lambda_{\text{spatial}}\cdot\rho_{\text{spatial}}^{(m)}-\lambda_{\text{temp}}\cdot\gamma_{\text{avg}}^{(m)}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>MAS</mtext><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>spatial</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mtext>spatial</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>temp</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mtext>avg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{MAS}_{m}=1-\beta_{m}\cdot\left(1-\lambda_{\text{spatial}}\cdot\rho_{\text{spatial}}^{(m)}-\lambda_{\text{temp}}\cdot\gamma_{\text{avg}}^{(m)}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{\text{spatial}}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mtext>spatial</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{\text{spatial}}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{\text{avg}}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mtext>avg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{\text{avg}}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math> are the spatial and temporal sparsity measures specific to modality <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> (applicable only when the modality exhibits such dimensions), and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{spatial}},\lambda_{\text{temp}}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>spatial</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>temp</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{spatial}},\lambda_{\text{temp}}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> are hyperparameters controlling the contribution of spatial and temporal redundancy. A high <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{MAS}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>MAS</mtext><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{MAS}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that modality <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> contains substantial redundancy or low task relevance, suggesting that it can be aggressively compressed or even omitted during inference. Conversely, a low <math id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{MAS}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>MAS</mtext><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{MAS}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> signals critical information that must be preserved with high fidelity. This lightweight probing module introduces minimal computational overhead, only requiring forward passes through early encoder layers and a few lightweight heads, yet provides fine-grained guidance for the subsequent edge-cloud offloading strategy. The computed MAS values, together with the spatial and temporal sparsity maps, serve as compact descriptors of the input’s computational characteristics, enabling adaptive resource allocation between edge and cloud.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02945v1/figure3.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/262;" width="476" height="262" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>The illustration of adaptive speculative edge-cloud collaborative offloading.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Adaptive Speculative Edge-Cloud Collaborative Offloading</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building upon the Modal Activation Sparsity (MAS) metrics derived from the lightweight probing module, we now address the core challenge of optimal offloading decisions in heterogeneous edge-cloud environments. Unlike conventional deterministic offloading strategies, we propose an adaptive speculative edge-cloud collaborative offloading mechanism, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1.4. Modal Activation Sparsity (MAS) ‣ 4.1. Lightweight Heterogeneous Modality-Aware via Fine-Grained Sparsity ‣ 4. The Proposed MSAO Framework ‣ MSAO: Adaptive Modality Sparsity-Aware Offloading with Edge-Cloud Collaboration for Efficient Multimodal LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. It leverages the inherent uncertainty in MLLM inference to minimize end-to-end latency while maintaining generation quality.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1. </span>Problem Formulation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider an MLLM inference task with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> input modalities, each characterized by its MAS value <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{MAS}_{m}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>MAS</mtext><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{MAS}_{m}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from the previous stage. The inference process consists of two phases: (1) <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prefill phase</span>, where input tokens are processed to generate the key-value (KV) cache, and (2) <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">decoding phase</span>, where tokens are generated autoregressively. We introduce a <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">speculative execution</span> framework where the edge device maintains a lightweight draft model (a subset of the MLLM layers) capable of generating <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">draft tokens</span> rapidly. The cloud server hosts the full MLLM for verification and correction. The key innovation lies in adaptively determining: (i) which modalities to offload, (ii) when to invoke speculative execution, and (iii) the confidence threshold that triggers asynchronous offloading.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{edge}}^{\text{prefill}}(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mtext>edge</mtext><mtext>prefill</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{edge}}^{\text{prefill}}(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the prefill latency on the edge, which depends on the retained modality proportions <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}=[\beta_{1},\ldots,\beta_{K}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}=[\beta_{1},\ldots,\beta_{K}]</annotation></semantics></math> and spatial-temporal compression ratios <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\rho}=[\rho_{1},\ldots,\rho_{K}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\rho}=[\rho_{1},\ldots,\rho_{K}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{cloud}}^{\text{prefill}}(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mtext>cloud</mtext><mtext>prefill</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{cloud}}^{\text{prefill}}(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math> represents cloud prefill latency. The communication latency for transmitting modality <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{comm}}^{(m)}=\frac{\text{DataSize}(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{m},\boldsymbol{\rho}_{m})}{B_{\text{eff}}}+RTT," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mtext>comm</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mtext>DataSize</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜷</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝆</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mtext>eff</mtext></msub></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{comm}}^{(m)}=\frac{\text{DataSize}(\boldsymbol{\beta}_{m},\boldsymbol{\rho}_{m})}{B_{\text{eff}}}+RTT,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\text{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mtext>eff</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\text{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the effective bandwidth and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="RTT" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">RTT</annotation></semantics></math> is the round-trip time. For the decoding phase, we model speculative generation as follows. The edge draft model generates <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{draft}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{draft}}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens with latency <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{draft}}=N_{\text{draft}}\cdot t_{\text{draft}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{draft}}=N_{\text{draft}}\cdot t_{\text{draft}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{draft}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{draft}}</annotation></semantics></math> is per-token latency on the edge. The cloud verification latency for these <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{draft}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{draft}}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{verify}}=N_{\text{draft}}\cdot t_{\text{verify}}^{\text{cloud}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>verify</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mtext>verify</mtext><mtext>cloud</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{verify}}=N_{\text{draft}}\cdot t_{\text{verify}}^{\text{cloud}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The acceptance probability of draft tokens is governed by the model’s confidence distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Then, we define the confidence of the draft model at generation step <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> as the entropy of the output probability distribution:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p}_{i})=-\sum_{v\in\mathcal{V}}p_{i}(v)\log p_{i}(v)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p}_{i})=-\sum_{v\in\mathcal{V}}p_{i}(v)\log p_{i}(v),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is the vocabulary. A lower entropy indicates higher confidence. We introduce a <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">confidence threshold</span> <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{conf}}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{conf}}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Speculate}(i)=\begin{cases}\text{True},&amp;\text{if }\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p}_{i})\leq\theta_{\text{conf}}.\\
\text{False},&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Speculate</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>True</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>False</mtext><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Speculate}(i)=\begin{cases}\text{True},&amp;\text{if }\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p}_{i})\leq\theta_{\text{conf}}.\\
\text{False},&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p}_{i})&gt;\theta_{\text{conf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p}_{i})&gt;\theta_{\text{conf}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the edge immediately offloads the intermediate state to the cloud for high-quality generation. Our goal is to minimize the expected end-to-end latency <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{e2e}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>e2e</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{e2e}}</annotation></semantics></math> while ensuring that the generation quality degradation <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta Q</annotation></semantics></math> remains bounded. The decision variables include:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}\in[0,1]^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}\in[0,1]^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>: modality retention ratios</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\rho}\in[0,1]^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\rho}\in[0,1]^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>: compression ratios per modality</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{conf}}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{conf}}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>: confidence threshold</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{draft}}\in\mathbb{N}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{draft}}\in\mathbb{N}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>: speculative decoding length</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The optimization problem is formulated as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E11X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="5" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underset{\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\rho},\theta_{\text{conf}},N_{\text{draft}}}{\text{minimize}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mtext>minimize</mtext><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝜷</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝝆</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">θ</mi><mtext mathsize="0.710em">conf</mtext></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">N</mi><mtext mathsize="0.710em">draft</mtext></msub></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underset{\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\rho},\theta_{\text{conf}},N_{\text{draft}}}{\text{minimize}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11X.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[L_{\text{e2e}}(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\rho},\theta_{\text{conf}},N_{\text{draft}})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>e2e</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[L_{\text{e2e}}(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\rho},\theta_{\text{conf}},N_{\text{draft}})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E11Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{subject to}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>subject to</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{subject to}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xa.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta Q(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\rho})\leq\epsilon_{Q}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta Q(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\rho})\leq\epsilon_{Q},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E11Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{m=1}^{K}\text{Mem}_{\text{edge}}(\beta_{m},\rho_{m})\leq\text{Mem}_{\text{edge}}^{\max}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mtext>Mem</mtext><mtext>edge</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mtext>Mem</mtext><mtext>edge</mtext><mi>max</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{m=1}^{K}\text{Mem}_{\text{edge}}(\beta_{m},\rho_{m})\leq\text{Mem}_{\text{edge}}^{\max},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="3"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E11Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xc.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{\text{comm}}^{(m)}\leq T_{\text{max}}^{(m)},\quad\forall m\in\mathcal{M}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>comm</mtext></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>max</mtext></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{\text{comm}}^{(m)}\leq T_{\text{max}}^{(m)},\quad\forall m\in\mathcal{M},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="3"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E11Xd" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11Xd.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\beta_{m}\geq 1-\text{MAS}_{m},\quad\forall m\in\mathcal{M}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext>MAS</mtext><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\beta_{m}\geq 1-\text{MAS}_{m},\quad\forall m\in\mathcal{M},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright" colspan="3"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum tolerable quality degradation, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Mem}_{\text{edge}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Mem</mtext><mtext>edge</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Mem}_{\text{edge}}</annotation></semantics></math> is edge memory consumption, and the final constraint ensures that modalities with low MAS (high importance) cannot be aggressively compressed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The expected end-to-end latency comprises three components: prefill latency, speculative decoding latency, and offloading latency for low-confidence steps. Let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{conf}}(\theta_{\text{conf}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{conf}}(\theta_{\text{conf}})</annotation></semantics></math> be the probability that the model’s confidence exceeds the threshold at a given step, approximated by the empirical cumulative distribution of entropy values:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{\text{conf}}(\theta_{\text{conf}})=\frac{1}{N_{\text{steps}}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text{steps}}}\mathbb{1}\left[\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p}_{i})\leq\theta_{\text{conf}}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>steps</mtext></msub></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>steps</mtext></msub></munderover><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{conf}}(\theta_{\text{conf}})=\frac{1}{N_{\text{steps}}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text{steps}}}\mathbb{1}\left[\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p}_{i})\leq\theta_{\text{conf}}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">The expected number of tokens generated via speculative execution is:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[N_{\text{spec}}]=\sum_{t=1}^{\infty}t\cdot P_{\text{conf}}(\theta_{\text{conf}})^{t-1}(1-P_{\text{conf}}(\theta_{\text{conf}}))=\frac{1}{1-P_{\text{conf}}(\theta_{\text{conf}})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>spec</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[N_{\text{spec}}]=\sum_{t=1}^{\infty}t\cdot P_{\text{conf}}(\theta_{\text{conf}})^{t-1}(1-P_{\text{conf}}(\theta_{\text{conf}}))=\frac{1}{1-P_{\text{conf}}(\theta_{\text{conf}})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">The total expected latency is then:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E14X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[L_{\text{e2e}}]=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>e2e</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[L_{\text{e2e}}]=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max\left(D_{\text{edge}}^{\text{prefill}}(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\rho}),\;D_{\text{cloud}}^{\text{prefill}}(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\rho})+\sum_{m}\mathbb{1}_{\text{offload}}^{(m)}T_{\text{comm}}^{(m)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mtext>edge</mtext><mtext>prefill</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mtext>cloud</mtext><mtext>prefill</mtext></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝝆</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>m</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mn>𝟙</mn><mtext>offload</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mtext>comm</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max\left(D_{\text{edge}}^{\text{prefill}}(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\rho}),\;D_{\text{cloud}}^{\text{prefill}}(\boldsymbol{\beta},\boldsymbol{\rho})+\sum_{m}\mathbb{1}_{\text{offload}}^{(m)}T_{\text{comm}}^{(m)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E14Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\mathbb{E}[N_{\text{spec}}]\cdot\left(T_{\text{draft}}+P_{\text{conf}}(\theta_{\text{conf}})\cdot T_{\text{verify}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>spec</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>draft</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>verify</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\mathbb{E}[N_{\text{spec}}]\cdot\left(T_{\text{draft}}+P_{\text{conf}}(\theta_{\text{conf}})\cdot T_{\text{verify}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E14Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\left(1-P_{\text{conf}}(\theta_{\text{conf}})\right)\cdot T_{\text{offload}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>T</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>offload</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\left(1-P_{\text{conf}}(\theta_{\text{conf}})\right)\cdot T_{\text{offload}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{1}_{\text{offload}}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mn>𝟙</mn><mtext>offload</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{1}_{\text{offload}}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates whether modality <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> requires offloading, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{offload}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>offload</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{offload}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the latency for offloading intermediate states during low-confidence steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2. </span>Adaptive Speculative Edge-Cloud Collaborative Offloading Algorithm</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Directly solving the optimization problem in Eq. (11) is challenging due to its non-convex objective and the strong coupling among decision variables <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{conf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{conf}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{draft}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{draft}}</annotation></semantics></math>. To address this challenge, we propose an adaptive speculative edge-cloud collaborative offloading algorithm that iteratively optimizes these variables based on real-time system states, as presented in Algorithm 1. The algorithm operates on two distinct timescales to balance global optimality with runtime adaptability:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Coarse-grained optimization (per-request level):</span> Executed once per inference request, this phase determines the modality retention ratios <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜷</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>, compression ratios <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝆</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>, and initial offloading decisions using the MAS metrics derived from the lightweight probing module. These decisions remain fixed throughout the decoding process for a given request.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fine-grained adaptation (per-step level):</span> Performed at each decoding step, this phase dynamically adjusts the confidence threshold <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{conf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{conf}}</annotation></semantics></math> based on observed model confidence and triggers speculative execution when appropriate. This fast-timescale adaptation enables the system to respond to varying input characteristics and real-time network conditions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Adaptive Speculative Edge-Cloud Collaborative Offloading Algorithm</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
              MAS values <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\text{MAS}_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mtext>MAS</mtext><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\text{MAS}_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, system state <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, quality bound <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
              Offloading decisions <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}^{*},\boldsymbol{\rho}^{*},\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*},N_{\text{draft}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mtext>draft</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}^{*},\boldsymbol{\rho}^{*},\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*},N_{\text{draft}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
              <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}^{*},\boldsymbol{\rho}^{*}\leftarrow\arg\min\mathbb{E}[L_{\text{prefill}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>prefill</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}^{*},\boldsymbol{\rho}^{*}\leftarrow\arg\min\mathbb{E}[L_{\text{prefill}}]</annotation></semantics></math> s.t. <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta Q\leq\epsilon_{Q},\ \beta_{m}\geq 1-\text{MAS}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mtext>MAS</mtext><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta Q\leq\epsilon_{Q},\ \beta_{m}\geq 1-\text{MAS}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
              <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}\leftarrow\mathcal{H}_{\text{emp}}^{-1}(0.7)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mtext>emp</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}\leftarrow\mathcal{H}_{\text{emp}}^{-1}(0.7)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
              <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{draft}}^{*}\leftarrow\min\left(\left\lfloor\frac{\log(1-P_{\text{target}})}{\log P_{\text{conf}}}\right\rfloor,N_{\text{max}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mtext>draft</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{draft}}^{*}\leftarrow\min\left(\left\lfloor\frac{\log(1-P_{\text{target}})}{\log P_{\text{conf}}}\right\rfloor,N_{\text{max}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each decoding step <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
                
                
                Compute draft token <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}^{\text{draft}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}^{\text{draft}}</annotation></semantics></math> and confidence <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p}_{i})\leq\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p}_{i})\leq\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{cache}\geq N_{\text{draft}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>cache</mtext><mo>≥</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mtext>draft</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{cache}\geq N_{\text{draft}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
                
                
                 Send cache to cloud for parallel verification

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
                
                
                 Update <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with EMA of accepted tokens

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span> <span id="alg1.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p}_{i})&gt;\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐩</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(\mathbf{p}_{i})&gt;\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l11.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
                
                
                 Asynchronously offload intermediate state <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to cloud

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
                
                
                 <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}\leftarrow\max(\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}\cdot\delta,\ \theta_{\text{min}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}\leftarrow\max(\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}\cdot\delta,\ \theta_{\text{min}})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l14.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\beta}^{*},\boldsymbol{\rho}^{*},\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*},N_{\text{draft}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜷</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝝆</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mtext>draft</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\beta}^{*},\boldsymbol{\rho}^{*},\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*},N_{\text{draft}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The algorithm exhibits several desirable properties. For the coarse-grained optimization phase, we employ Bayesian optimization with Gaussian process surrogate models, which achieves sublinear regret:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[R_{T}]=O\left(\sqrt{T\cdot\log T}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[R_{T}]=O\left(\sqrt{T\cdot\log T}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of function evaluations and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the cumulative regret relative to the global optimum. For the fine-grained adaptation, the exponential moving average (EMA) update ensures that <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> converges to the optimal threshold that balances speculation success rate and offloading overhead:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathbb{E}[|\theta_{\text{conf}}^{(n)}-\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}|^{2}]=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{n\to\infty}\mathbb{E}[|\theta_{\text{conf}}^{(n)}-\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}|^{2}]=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Complexity Analysis.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The computational complexity of it is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(K\cdot\log K+N_{\text{steps}}\cdot M_{\text{draft}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>steps</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(K\cdot\log K+N_{\text{steps}}\cdot M_{\text{draft}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{draft}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{draft}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the draft model size, which is significantly smaller than the full MLLM complexity <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N_{\text{steps}}\cdot M_{\text{full}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>steps</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>full</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N_{\text{steps}}\cdot M_{\text{full}})</annotation></semantics></math>, achieving an acceleration factor of approximately <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{full}}/M_{\text{draft}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>full</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>draft</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{full}}/M_{\text{draft}}</annotation></semantics></math> for high-confidence steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1. </span>Datasets and Implementation Details</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our method on two widely adopted multimodal benchmarks: <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) VQAv2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goyal et al., 2017</a>)</cite>: A large-scale visual question answering dataset containing over 250,000 images and 1.1 million questions. We randomly sample 5,000 images from the validation set to construct our test set, ensuring diverse coverage of question types and visual content. <span id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) MMBench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: A comprehensive multimodal benchmark that evaluates model performance across 20 distinct capability dimensions, including object recognition, attribute reasoning, and scene understanding. We utilize the full test set for evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct experiments on a heterogeneous edge-cloud platform. The cloud server is equipped with an NVIDIA A100 (40 GB) GPU, while the edge device is configured with an NVIDIA RTX 3090 (24 GB) GPU.
We deploy a lightweight draft model on the edge device and a full-capacity model on the cloud. Specifically, we adopt Qwen2-VL-2B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2023</a>)</cite> as the edge-side draft model, which features 2 billion parameters and is capable of rapid token generation. The cloud-side model is Qwen2.5-VL-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, a 7-billion-parameter multimodal large language model that delivers high-quality generation. The two models share the same tokenizer and architectural design, enabling seamless speculative verification. To simulate realistic edge-cloud communication scenarios, we configure the network bandwidth at three representative levels: 200 Mbps (low-bandwidth), 300 Mbps (medium-bandwidth), and 400 Mbps (high-bandwidth). The round-trip time (RTT) is fixed at 20 ms to reflect typical edge-cloud latency. All reported results are averaged over three independent runs to ensure statistical reliability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2. </span>Baseline Methods</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare our proposed MSAO framework against the following baseline approaches. <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Cloud-only</span>: The full Qwen2.5-VL-7B model runs entirely on the cloud server. All multimodal inputs are transmitted to the cloud for inference. <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2) Edge-only</span>: The lightweight Qwen2-VL-2B model runs entirely on the edge device. <span id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3) PerLLM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: An edge-cloud collaborative inference framework that partitions MLLM execution based on layer-wise offloading.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.3. </span>Evaluation Metrics</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the proposed method using the following metrics:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy</span>: For VQAv2, we report the standard VQA accuracy, which measures the exact match between predicted answers and ground-truth annotations. For MMBench, we report the average accuracy across all 20 capability dimensions. These metrics assess the generation quality preservation under edge-cloud collaboration.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Throughput</span>: The number of tokens generated per second (tokens/s), which reflects the system’s processing efficiency under continuous inference workloads.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">End-to-End Latency</span>: The total time from when the input is submitted to when the final response is fully generated. This includes prefill phase latency, decoding phase latency, and all communication overheads. We report the average latency per inference request (in milliseconds).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computing Overhead</span>: This metric quantifies the total floating-point operations (FLOPs) consumed during inference, capturing the computational burden on both edge and cloud devices.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Memory Overhead</span>: This metric measures the peak GPU memory consumption during inference, including model weights, activations, and KV cache occupancy.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.4. </span>Parameter Configuration</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The spatial sparsity threshold <math id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0.3 based on empirical sensitivity analysis. The temporal redundancy weight <math id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{temp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>temp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{temp}}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0.4, and the spatial redundancy weight <math id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{spatial}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>spatial</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{spatial}}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0.6, reflecting the observation that spatial redundancy often contributes more to overall computational savings than temporal redundancy in image-based tasks. The maximum tolerable quality degradation <math id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 2% relative to the Cloud-only baseline, ensuring that the proposed method maintains generation fidelity. The initial confidence threshold <math id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mtext>conf</mtext><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{conf}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to the 70th percentile of the empirical entropy distribution collected from a calibration set of 500 samples. The threshold decay factor <math id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0.95, balancing adaptation speed and stability. The maximum speculative length <math id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 5, consistent with prior work on speculative decoding. For coarse-grained optimization, we perform 50 iterations of Bayesian optimization using a Gaussian process surrogate with Matérn 5/2 kernel. The acquisition function is expected to improve with an exploration-exploitation trade-off parameter of 0.1. The target acceptance probability <math id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{target}}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0.8, providing a balance between speculation aggressiveness and verification overhead.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.02945v1/ganzhi.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/286;" width="476" height="286" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>The performance analysis of lightweight heterogeneous modality-aware processing.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Lightweight Heterogeneous Modality-Aware Analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the lightweight nature of our proposed modality-aware module, we conduct a comprehensive analysis of its computational characteristics under varying configurations. Specifically, we report results for seven representative configurations (V1–V7), covering unimodal text, bimodal image-text, and trimodal video-text-audio inputs across increasing resolution and sequence length.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.1.4. Parameter Configuration ‣ 5.1. Experimental Setup ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ MSAO: Adaptive Modality Sparsity-Aware Offloading with Edge-Cloud Collaboration for Efficient Multimodal LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the average inference latency contributed by the aware module ranges from 4.2 ms to 15.3 ms, depending on input complexity, which constitutes less than 2% of the total end-to-end inference latency in typical edge-cloud deployment scenarios. Notably, even under the most demanding configuration, the module completes its analysis within 15.3 ms, ensuring that it does not become a bottleneck in the inference pipeline. In terms of computing overhead, the module introduces a minimal FLOPs increase of only 0.47% to 1.23% relative to the full MLLM inference pipeline, demonstrating its highly efficient design. Specifically, the spatial aware head accounts for the majority of this overhead, while the temporal and modal aware components contribute negligible additional computation due to their reliance on lightweight hash operations and MLP projections. In terms of memory overhead, the aware module adds only 0.12 GB to 0.28 GB of additional GPU memory footprint on the edge device. This marginal increase is primarily attributed to the storage of intermediate feature maps from the early encoder layers, which are already resident in memory for subsequent processing. The spatial importance maps and temporal similarity caches are stored as lightweight tensors, contributing negligible memory pressure. Collectively, these results confirm that the proposed lightweight modality-aware module achieves its design objective of providing fine-grained sparsity analysis without imposing significant computational, memory, or latency burdens on the overall system.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Main Results and Analysis</h3>

<section id="S5.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.3.1. </span>Accuracy Comparison</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.3.1. Accuracy Comparison ‣ 5.3. Main Results and Analysis ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ MSAO: Adaptive Modality Sparsity-Aware Offloading with Edge-Cloud Collaboration for Efficient Multimodal LLM Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the proposed MSAO framework achieves accuracy comparable to the cloud-only upper bound while significantly outperforming edge-only and PerLLM baselines. Specifically, MSAO maintains a minimal performance gap of less than 0.4% relative to the cloud-only approach across both datasets and various bandwidth conditions (e.g., 76.1% vs. 76.3% on VQAv2 at 200 Mbps, and 76.3% vs. 76.5% on MMBench at 400 Mbps). In contrast, MSAO substantially surpasses the edge-only and PerLLM methods by large margins, with absolute accuracy improvements ranging from 4.8% to 16.8% across all settings. These results demonstrate that MSAO effectively preserves the high accuracy of cloud-based inference through adaptive heterogeneous offloading, while achieving substantial gains over purely edge-based or suboptimal adaptive strategies.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Accuracy (%) comparison results.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:129.2pt;vertical-align:-61.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(23.0pt,-8.6pt) scale(1.15352791511719,1.15352791511719) ;">
<table id="S5.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></td>
